   "sentenceId": 2,
   "he": "גְּמָ' תָּנָא: דּוֹר קָטוּעַ. תָּנֵי רַבִּי חִיָּיא: כְּשֵׁם שֶׁהַשְּׂאוֹר יָפֶה לָעִיסָּה, כָּךְ דָּמִים יָפִין לָאִשָּׁה. תָּנָא מִשּׁוּם רַבִּי מֵאִיר: כׇּל אִשָּׁה שֶׁדָּמֶיהָ מְרוּבִּין — בָּנֶיהָ מְרוּבִּין.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» With regard to the term durkati, the Gemara explains that this means «truncated generation [dor katua].» As «Rabbi Ḥiyya teaches: Just as leaven is good for dough, so too, blood is good for a woman.» It was likewise «taught in the name of Rabbi Meir: Any woman whose blood is plentiful, her children are plentiful.»"
  }
